1. Global Dashboard Standards
1.1 Data freshness and status labels
Every page, section, card, table, and graph must show the actual data date and the last successful refresh date/time. A single page-level date is not enough when sections update on different schedules.
Current: The source updated within its approved freshness window.
Delayed: The source is inside a known platform processing lag, such as Amazon Seller Central’s normal 24–48-hour delay.
Stale: The source missed its approved update window. Display the label prominently and show how old the data is.
Missing: The required field or file is absent. Do not replace it with zero, a dash, or a healthy status.
Manual: The value depends on a person updating a file. Show the owner and last confirmed date.
Authentication required: A login, MFA challenge, expired session, or permission problem blocked the pull.
Recommended wording: Use “STALE” rather than “Stagnate.” It is shorter, standard dashboard language, and clearly describes old data.
1.2 Header refresh display
Delete the “Auto-refresh in” countdown.
At the top of the dashboard, display “Last automatic refresh: July 24, 2026, 7:04 AM PT.”
Also display “Last successful full run” and “Last attempted run” when they differ.
A failed run must show a red error banner with the failed source and error time.
The manual Refresh button can remain, but it must not make old source data appear current.
1.3 Missing-data behavior and health status
Do not show GREEN when any critical KPI is missing. The Business Health screenshot shows GREEN while MTD Revenue, MTD Net Income, Amazon Net Profit, Shopify Sales, and Total Customers have no data.
Eliminate null, undefined, NaN, and placeholder output. The “null% top-tier” text must never appear.
When a value cannot be calculated, show “No data” plus the reason, source, owner, and last successful date.
Calculate the page health status from the worst critical-source status, not from whichever fields happened to load.
Add a completeness indicator such as “8 of 11 critical sources current.”
1.4 Product master and new products
Remove hard-coded references to “all 6 active SKUs.” All product-level sections must read from one product master so new products automatically appear everywhere that tracks individual products.
